  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The first of several pieces of energy has \nmoved through NV bringing snow across much of the area as \ntemperatures came in below forecast last night. Winds are still \ngoing to be elevated through the rest of the evening, as gusty S \nto SW winds will be possible through Friday morning. Especially \nfor central Nevada as N Nye, White Pine, southern Lander, and S \nEureka Counties could see South to Southwest winds of 20 to 30 MPH\nwith gusts up to 40 MPH possible. Further north winds aren\u2019t \nexpected to be as strong, but will still be breezy at 15 to 25 MPH\nwith gust up to 35 MPH possible. The combination of winds and \nsnowfall will bring adverse to hazardous driving conditions for \narea highways through Friday. Winter headlines continue for area \nwith a Winter Storm Warning for the Ruby Mountains, and a winter \nWeather Advisory for parts of Elko, Eureka, Lander, Humboldt, and\nWhite Pine Counties through Friday evening. \n\nFriday a cold front will push through the region which will shift\nwind to the NW with breezy conditions continuing with speeds of \n10 to 20 MPH with gusts up the 30 MPH possible. Also for Friday \nthe main upper trough will pass overhead, and with that will be \nthe possibility for isolated to scattered convective snow showers \nand snow squalls which would offer less overall precipitation \ncoverage, but give enhanced precipitation rates. These events \ncould bring a boost to local snow totals. Central Nevada will see \nthe greatest chances for these features as instability, what \nlittle there is will be concentrated along the US 50 corridor. \nTemperatures for this event will likely depend on precipitation \nmechanics as areas with active precipitation will be cooler due to\nwet bulb effects, only to rebound as breaks in precipitation \noccur. Overall highs will range in the low 30s to low 40s with \nlows in the upper 20s through Saturday morning. \n\nWith the exit of this upper trough, Saturday will see a brief \nbreak as a short wave ridge passes through. This break however \nwill be short lived as another upper level trough will start to \nmove through Sunday afternoon through Monday evening. This system \nlooks to be weaker and faster than the late week storm, but could\nhave the potential to bring isolated rain/snow showers with \naccumulations of 0.5\u201d to 2\u201d of snow for the passes and range \nsummits, and up to 0.10\u201d of rain for the valleys. Tuesday will \nbring another quick break in the action before another system will\ntry to move through for next Wednesday with more chances for \nrain/snow showers. Temperature for the rest of the week look to \nremain near to or below normal as highs look to range in the upper\n30s to upper 40s with lows in the upper teens to 20s for \novernight lows.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Moderate confidence continues\nfor precipitation though a quicker end to precipitation on Friday\nnow seems more likely. High confidence exists in coldest\ntemperatures of the period taking place Saturday night. Moderate\nconfidence exists in the continuation of an active pattern into\nnext week. However, confidence is low so far in precip type,\ntiming, and amounts.",
